where, $E_0=$ amplitude of electric field.
Here,
$\begin{aligned}
I & =\frac{15}{\pi} \frac{\mathrm{W}}{\mathrm{m}^2}, \frac{1}{4 \pi \varepsilon_0}=9 \times 10^9 \frac{\mathrm{Nm}^2}{\mathrm{C}^2} \\
c & =3 \times 10^8 \mathrm{~ms}^{-1}
\end{aligned}$
From Eq. (i), we get
$\begin{aligned}
& E_0^2=\frac{2 \times I}{\varepsilon_0 \times c}=\frac{2 \times 4 \pi \times I}{4 \pi \varepsilon_0 \times c}=\left(\frac{2 \times 4 \pi \times \frac{15}{\pi} \times 9 \times 10^9}{3 \times 10^8}\right) \\
\Rightarrow & E_0^2=3600 \Rightarrow E_0=60 \mathrm{~N} / \mathrm{C}
\end{aligned}$

The maximum kinetic energy of an electron accelerated through a potential difference of $\mathrm{V}$ volt is $\frac{1}{2} \mathrm{mv}^{2}=\mathrm{eV}$
\begin{array}{l}
\therefore \text { maximum velocity } \mathrm{v}=\sqrt{\frac{2 \mathrm{eV}}{\mathrm{m}}} \\
\mathrm{v}=\sqrt{\frac{2 \times 1.6 \times 10^{-19} \times 15000}{9.1 \times 10^{-31}}} \\
\mathrm{v}=7.26 \times 10^{7} \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{s}
\end{array}

Given, refractive index of the medium, $n=15$
Permeability of the medium,

\mu=5 \times 10^{-7} \mathrm{Hm}^{-1}

Relative permeability of the medium,

\mu_{r}=\frac{\mu}{\mu_{0}}=\frac{5 \times 10^{-7}}{4 \pi \times 10^{-7}}=\frac{5}{4 \pi}

We know that, refractive index,

\begin{aligned}
n &=\sqrt{\varepsilon_{r} \mu_{r}} \\
n^{2} &=\varepsilon_{r} \mu_{r} \\
\Rightarrow \quad \varepsilon_{r} &=\frac{n^{2}}{\mu_{r}}=\frac{(1.5)^{2}}{5 / 4 \pi} \\
&=\frac{(1.5)^{2} \times 4 \pi}{5}=5.7 \simeq 6
\end{aligned}
